course: dictionary
lesson: 2835
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. Divvy is a good example. In the sentence «Div» and «divvy» mean idiot, divvy is an insult, not a way of sharing.
explain: Divvy is also a verb. To divvy up something means to share it out between people, as in "Let's divvy up the sweets so everyone gets the same." Ask yourself: is the sentence about sharing, or about a person being silly?
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If "Let's share out the sweets" still makes sense, you have chosen the right meaning. As an insult, divvy is mild playground language in the UK, but it is not kind, so use it with care.
fact: Divvy has more than one meaning, so check the context to find the right one.
fact: «Div» and «divvy» mean idiot. In UK slang, divvy is a mild insult for a silly or foolish person.
fact: To divvy up something means to share it out between people.
fact: As an older informal word, a divvy is a share of money, or a dividend.
fact: In maths, divvy is linked to division and fair shares.
exercise: "Let's divvy up the sweets so everyone gets the same." What does divvy up mean here? | *To share them out between people | To count them twice | To eat them all quickly | To hide them in a bag
exercise: Jamie says, "Don't be such a divvy!" What does divvy mean? | *A silly or foolish person | A share of the money | To share something out | A large dog
exercise: «Nah yeah» means yes. What does nah yeah mean? | Stop it | *Yes | No | Maybe
test: "Can you divvy up the job between the two of you?" What does this mean? | To forget the job | To give the job to a dog | To finish the job alone | *To share the job out
test: A playground friend calls you a divvy. What kind of word is divvy in that case? | A type of cake | A kind compliment | A sports team | *A mild insult
exam: What is the best way to check which meaning of divvy fits a sentence? | Skip the whole sentence | Guess the first meaning you think of | Look it up and stop reading | *Put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
